WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:09.516
Eleven forty p.m., a diagnostic lab in Nashik. A staff member opens an email attachment called GST notice dot zip.

2
00:00:09.816 --> 00:00:15.399
By morning, every computer on the network has locked its files, and a ransom note is waiting.

3
00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:20.834
Two weeks of patient reports. They want two lakh rupees in crypto.

4
00:00:21.234 --> 00:00:28.106
First response: unplug the network cable and any USB drives. Do not switch the computer off.

5
00:00:28.406 --> 00:00:36.395
Photograph the ransom note, and note the file extension and the time. Never send the money: it does not guarantee your files.

6
00:00:36.795 --> 00:00:43.437
The lab had a backup kept offline. It restored every report, and reported to CERT-In and the police.

7
00:00:43.837 --> 00:00:49.505
That is the three two one rule: three copies, on two kinds of media, with one kept offline.

8
00:00:50.305 --> 00:00:56.587
Phones get malware too. A message says: traffic challan pending, install this update.

9
00:00:56.887 --> 00:01:04.366
The app asks for SMS and Accessibility permission. Now it can read your OTPs, and tap the screen for you.

10
00:01:04.666 --> 00:01:11.473
Warning signs: a hot, slow phone, OTPs you did not request, and friends getting messages you never sent.

11
00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:19.301
Switch on airplane mode, uninstall the app, and call your bank. Then secure your email first: it is the master key.

12
00:01:19.701 --> 00:01:28.030
Free cleaning tools are on C S K dot gov dot in. Disconnect first, think second. A backup is the real cure.
